1. Function and Parameter
Definition:

A function (also called a method in Java) is a block of code designed to perform a specific task. It helps organize a program, avoid repeating code, and make the program easier to understand.

A parameter is a variable declared inside a method's parentheses. It receives a value that can be used inside the method.

code 

public class functionex {

    // Function with a parameter
    static void greet(String name) {
        System.out.println("Hello, " + name + "!");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        greet("Pio");
        greet("Tristan");

    }
}

2. Arrays in JAVA
Definition:

An array is a collection of elements of the same data type stored in a single variable. Each element has an index number, starting from 0.

Arrays are useful when storing multiple values without creating separate variables for each one.

code 

public class arrayex {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Declare and initialize an array
        int[] grades = {90, 85, 88, 92, 95};

        // Display array elements
        for (int i = 0; i < grades.length; i++) {
            System.out.println("Grade " + (i + 1) + ": " + grades[i]);
        }

    }
}

3. What are break(), continue(), and return()?
These are Java control statements used to control the flow of program execution.
A. break
Definition:

The break statement is used to immediately stop a loop or exit a switch statement, even if the condition is still true.


code 


public class breakex {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {

            if (i == 3) {
                break;
            }

            System.out.println(i);
        }

    }
}



B. continue
Definition:

The continue statement skips the current iteration of a loop and proceeds to the next iteration.

code


public class ContinueExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {

            if (i == 3) {
                continue;
            }

            System.out.println(i);
        }

    }
}


C. return
Definition:

The return statement is used to end the execution of a method. It can also send a value back to the method that called it.


code 

public class ReturnExample {

    // Method that returns a value
    static int addNumbers(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int result = addNumbers(10, 20);

        System.out.println("The sum is: " + result);

    }
}


BREAK = Stop the loop.



CONTINUE = Skip and move on.



RETURN = Exit the method and give back a result (if applicable).
